# Release Notes for *3.0.9*
Tue 7 Jul 2026 20:01:15

## UI Revision
Refreshing lighter colors, better UI elements, less blocky panels...

```wolfram
NumericArray[{1,2,4}]
```

```wolfram
Panel[1+1]
```

```wolfram
Quantity[1, "Meters"/"Kilograms"]
```

## Rows, Columns, Grids, and Tables
We significantly improved support for the standard symbols used to format lists and tables. For `Grid`, `Row`, and `Column`, the `Dividers`, `Spacings`, `Alignment`, `ImageSize`, and `BaselinePosition` options are now supported. For `TableForm`, `TableAlignments`, `TableHeadings`, and `TableSpacing` are supported as well. Here are a few examples:

```wolfram
Row[{
  Graphics[{Red, Disk[]}, ImageSize->{18,18}, PlotRange->2], 
  Style["Hey there", (RGBColor[0.3333333333333333, 0.3333333333333333, 0.3333333333333333])]
}, Alignment->{{Right, Left},Center}, ImageSize->400]
```

Grid examples

```wolfram
Grid[Table[x, {3}, {5}],  Spacings->{5,0}]
```

```wolfram
Grid[Table[x, {3}, {7}], 
 Dividers -> {{False, False, True}, {False, True}}, Spacings->2]
```

```wolfram
Grid[{
  {Graphics[Rectangle[{-1,-1}, {1,1}], ImageSize -> 20], 
   Graphics[Rectangle[{-1,-1}, {1,1}], ImageSize -> 30]}, 
  {Graphics[Rectangle[{-1,-1}, {1,1}],ImageSize -> 40], 
   Graphics[Rectangle[{-1,-1}, {1,1}], ImageSize -> 50]}}, 
 Alignment -> {{Right, Left}, {Bottom, Top}}]
```

Baseline alignment

```wolfram
a Grid[{{Orange}, {Yellow}}, BaselinePosition->Top]
a Grid[{{Orange}, {Yellow}}, BaselinePosition->Center]
```

Column examples

```wolfram
Column[{1, 12, 123, 1234}, Center]
```

```wolfram
Column[{1, 22, 333}, Dividers -> All]
```

Table examples

```wolfram
TableForm[{{5, 7}, {4, 2}, {10, 3}}, 
 TableHeadings -> {{"Group A", "Group B", "Group C"}, {"y1", "y2"}}]
```

Note that this can still be used as input.

```wolfram
data = {{(None),"y1","y2"},{"Group A",5,7},{"Group B",4,2},{"Group C",10,3}};

data[[2,2]]
```

Styling options

```wolfram
ScientificForm[TableForm[RandomReal[10^5, {2, 3}]], 3]
```

Styled cells

```wolfram
Grid[{{Item[1, Background -> Red, Frame -> True], 2}, {3, 4}}]
```

MatrixForm output

```wolfram
MatrixForm @ PauliMatrix[3]
```

For tables that need lazy-loading support:

```wolfram
RandomReal[10^5, {2000, 3}]//TableView  
```

---

## `Deploy` support
The Wolfram Language `Deploy` wrapper now works with any decorated expression. It:
- traverses the full expression tree
- makes the expression non-selectable and non-editable

A typical use case is constructing a GUI with `Panel`, `Row`, and `Grid` blocks:

```wolfram
Row[{
  Graphics[{Red, Disk[]}, ImageSize->{18,18}, PlotRange->2], 
  Style["Hey there", (RGBColor[0.3333333333333333, 0.3333333333333333, 0.3333333333333333])]
}, Alignment->{{Right, Left},Center}, ImageSize->400] // Deploy
```

The earlier example is now unselectable and uneditable.

## Improved `Panel`
`Panel` now supports background and appearance options.

```wolfram
Panel[Plot[x, {x,0,1}], Background->LightYellow]
Panel[Plot[x, {x,0,1}], Appearance->"Frameless"]
```

## `StandardForm` update
As you might know, not every expression can be placed on a slide or WLX cell - only if it has `WLXForm` defined or they might also look different. You can bypass it by forcing `StandardForm` on it.

Here is an example:

```wolfram
Expr = Panel[1/2]
ExprForSlide = StandardForm[Expr];
```

<dummy >FrontEndExecutable[ba89cb44-04a0-4060-8bbe-9c1967a41efd]</dummy>

```jsx

<ExprForSlide/>
```

The same will work out of the box for all `Deploy`-able expressions too.

## `Locator` Primitive
We added `Locator[]` symbol for easier 2D or 3D coordinates manipulation, here is an example:

```wolfram
location = {0.3,0.7};
Plot[x, {x,0,1}, Epilog->{
  EventHandler[Locator[location], {
    "drag"->Function[xy, location = xy]
  }]
}]

Refresh[NumberForm[location, {3,2}], 1/2]
```

As you can see it generates `drag` event as `Disk`, `Polygon`, `Text` and `Rectangle`.

## Improved `TextView`
`TextView` now applies fewer default styles and supports multiple lines, as well as the `ImageSize` option:

```wolfram
TextView["Hey!"]
```

```wolfram
TextView["Hey!\nThere"]
```

```wolfram
TextView["A very long line here", ImageSize->100]
```

## Frontend support for string operators
We extended the lightweight WL interpreter that runs in the frontend with more string-related operators, including `ToString`, `NumberForm`, `StringJoin`, and `Round`. This is useful for frontend-side formatting and label generation.

```wolfram
sqval = 0.3;
EventHandler[InputRange[0.33,1,0.1], (sqval=Sqrt[#])&]

TextView[
  sqval // Offload,
  "Label" -> "Before"
]

TextView[
  StringJoin["sqval = ", 
    ToString[NumberForm[sqval, {4,2}]
  ]] // Offload,
  "Label" -> "After"
]
```

These operators also work under `Offload`, because they are defined as frontend symbols.

Here is another example that uses `Round`.

```wolfram
ln = Table[{i, 5.0 Sin[i/25.0 Pi]}, {i,0,50,0.04}];
Graphics[{
  Red, Line[ln//Offload],
  Blue, Line[ln//Round//Offload],
  EventHandler[AnimationFrameListener[ln//Offload], (
    ln = Transpose[{ln[[All,1]], RotateLeft[ln[[All,2]], 1]}]
  )&]
}, ImageSize->150]
```

## Improved support for packed arrays
We improved how packed arrays are stored and transferred from the Wolfram Kernel to the frontend. During initial evaluation, `Graphics` and `Image` expressions are usually serialized as `"ExpressionJSON"` and reinterpreted in the frontend; subsequent updates use binary WXF. That initial pass was inefficient because numeric and packed arrays were serialized as plain lists by default, increasing payload sizes by 10-100 times. We patched the standard JSON serializer so packed and numeric arrays are sent as base64-encoded WXF strings. This matters especially for data structures such as `Image` and `Graphics3D` objects with many vertices.

```wolfram
ComplexPlot3D[((Power[Sin[z],3])/(Power[(z+1),4])), {z, -5 - 5 I, 5 + 5 I}]
```

As before, the data is also compressed with gzip. This should reduce file size, memory use, and CPU usage.

## Association keys for frontend symbols
Frontend symbols that are associations now support key access. Previously, you could do this:

```wolfram
h = {1,2}; 
TextView[h[[1]]//Offload]
```

Because `[[1]]` implicitly calls the `Part` operator, `a["key"]` was previously treated as a function-style argument instead of association key access.

```wolfram
a = <|"a"->1, "b"->0.3|>;
TextView[a["a"]//Offload]
TextView[a["b"]//Offload]
```

Now `a["key"]` works when `a` is an `Association`. Try it yourself!

```wolfram
a = <|"a"->-1, "b"->0.8|>;
```

